2186102144 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445287 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186105855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445291 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186105855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445292 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186105856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445293 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106040 1071 "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t) = 3\\cos ti + 4\\sin tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$n = (a(t)i + b(t)j)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)$.\nFind the function $a(t)$." 13194139534948 17592186045417 false] [17592186106040 1071 "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106040 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106043 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t) = 4\\sin ti + 8\\cos tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$n = (a(t)i + b(t)j)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)$.\nFind the function $a(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139534948 17592186045417 false] [17592186106043 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186106043 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445294 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445295 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445296 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445297 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445298 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534948 17592186045417 true] [17592186445262 1045 "Outward-Pointing Unit N